function page(name, title, kicker, content) {
  return Object.freeze({ name, title, kicker, content });
}

const systemGuide = page(
  "index.html",
  "System Guide",
  "From natural-language material to replayable semantic answers",
  `<p class="lead">nllAgent is a dependency-free Node.js workbench that turns a natural-language request and its source material into inspectable semantic programs, executes those programs, and returns a grounded Markdown CNL answer. It is designed for the awkward middle ground between an ordinary language model and a conventional rules engine: the request may be ambiguous enough to require a coding agent, while the resulting analysis must be explicit enough to test, replay, and review.</p>
<div class="callout"><strong>The central promise.</strong> On first encounter, Codex may interpret natural language and author code. Once that code is accepted, ordinary execution uses the committed <code>.mjs</code> programs and the deterministic runtime; it does not depend on an unrecorded model conversation. The <a href="specsLoader.html?spec=DS000-vision.md">vision contract</a> fixes this boundary, and the <a href="specsLoader.html?spec=matrix.md">specification browser</a> exposes the complete design.</div>

<h2>Begin with the question the operator is actually asking</h2>
<p>Consider the cold-chain tutorial. The operator is not merely asking the system to find words such as “calibration” or “acknowledgement.” The request is to decide whether a release conclusion is justified by a policy and a concrete transfer record. The source contains requirements, measurements, an expired certificate, an acknowledgement by one party, the explicit absence of acknowledgement by another party, and a conclusion that the sample may be released. A useful answer must keep those roles separate. It must know which sentences state policy, which state observed facts, which deny a fact, which sentence is the conclusion under review, and which missing information should remain unknown.</p>
<p>A single opaque completion can produce a plausible paragraph, but it leaves several questions unanswered. Which source passage justified each conclusion? Did absence of a matching sentence become falsehood by accident? Was a policy requirement confused with an event that actually occurred? Would the same interpretation be used on a second run? Could a programmer reuse the cold-chain vocabulary without also reusing the facts about sample AX-17? nllAgent addresses those questions by making the interpretation itself a set of executable artifacts.</p>

<h2>The request contains different kinds of language</h2>
<p>The first distinction is between language that teaches reusable behavior and language that belongs to one task. An <em>agent brief</em> describes a durable specialty: the concepts an agent should recognize, the checks it should perform across many sources, the evidence discipline it should follow, and the kinds of answer it should produce. A <em>task instruction</em> selects an operation for one run, such as finding contradictions, checking evidence for a conclusion, or generating an operational procedure. The <em>task sources</em> are the documents whose statements become evidence. They do not share the same semantic status.</p>
<p>The instruction is never inserted into the document as though the document had asserted it. The source is never promoted into default world knowledge merely because one task used it. Reusable distinctions belong to framework packs or the agent folder; source-grounded claims belong to the task. This ownership rule is what lets one reviewed rule-review agent serve many documents without contaminating later tasks with earlier facts. The <a href="project-structure.html">folder chapter</a> follows that boundary through the repository.</p>

<h2>The first encounter leaves programs behind</h2>
<p>When the required programs do not exist, nllAgent prepares a coding run rather than pretending that deterministic ingestion understands the document. It resolves the target agent and task, decodes the sources, determines the authoring phase, installs the dependency-closed skill set, and derives compact catalogs from the live SDK, ontology stack, circuit registry, profile, sources, and diagnostics. Codex reads that context and edits canonical JavaScript modules directly. The adapter retains the process, but the natural-language final message from Codex is not treated as the result of the analysis.</p>
<p>Agent-level authoring may create reusable OntologyJS, CircuitJS, response policy, profiles, and tests from an agent brief. Task-level authoring creates IntentJS and LongTextJS from the instruction and source, adding local ontology or circuits only where inherited semantics are genuinely insufficient. These phases are separated because “the source says this happened” and “every future task should understand this kind of event” are different commitments. The <a href="agentic-authoring.html">authoring chapter</a> explains the complete lifecycle; <a href="coding-agent.html">the coding-agent chapter</a> opens the adapter, skills, context, direct-editing, and verification boundary in detail.</p>

<h2>The semantic programs divide interpretation into reviewable responsibilities</h2>
<p><strong>OntologyJS</strong> supplies the vocabulary and the rules for constructing well-typed semantic values. In the cold-chain case it defines what a custody transfer, calibration validity, acknowledgement, temperature excursion, and release conclusion are, along with the roles that connect them. It does not say that a particular thermometer is valid or that Vale Laboratory acknowledged anything. Ontology code establishes the signature in which those claims can later be expressed.</p>
<p><strong>LongTextJS</strong> is the task-owned interpretation of the source. It constructs the particular transfer, readings, thermometer, parties, acknowledgements, policy requirements, and conclusion; it attaches polarity, modality, scope, and exact source spans. “No acknowledgement by Vale Laboratory was attached” becomes a grounded denied acknowledgement rather than a missing keyword. “The calibration certificate expired” becomes a grounded fact about validity at the transfer time. The distinction between a source claim and a pack fact remains visible.</p>
<p><strong>IntentJS</strong> is the executable interpretation of what the operator asked to do. It selects the analysis or generation mode, semantic concerns, evidence expectations, assurance, fallback behavior, requested outputs, and presentation policy. The same LongTextJS snapshot could be used by an intent that audits release support, another that checks only measurement normalization, or another that generates a corrective procedure. Intent is therefore a plan demand, not a label guessed from the document.</p>
<p><strong>CircuitJS</strong> supplies source-independent behavior. A circuit queries semantic terms, normalizes values, evaluates explicit conditions, preserves unknown and conflict states, and emits findings or typed generation frames with evidence. It reads the semantic store rather than searching raw prose. A response circuit runs later and decides which already-established results should appear, how they are grouped, which exact evidence is quoted, and which controlled-language templates express them. The detailed language surfaces are documented under <a href="semantic-dsls.html">Semantic programs</a>.</p>

<h2>The runtime turns the separate programs into one analysis</h2>
<p>Ontology and LongText modules execute through a semantic transaction. The transaction validates sorts, role cardinalities, scope, provenance, interpretation compatibility, and coverage before it commits anything. Accepted values enter one logical <code>SemanticStore</code>. The store may maintain specialized indexes for types, roles, claims, spans, time, quantities, identity, and provenance, but those indexes are implementation views of one semantic authority. A circuit sees typed handles and queries, not storage fields.</p>
<p>IntentJS then becomes a capability demand. The planner examines every resolved circuit from mandatory framework knowledge, the selected profile, the reusable agent, and the task. It rejects incompatible providers, closes required capabilities recursively, detects missing providers or cycles, and records why each candidate was selected, rejected, or blocked. The selected plan is sometimes called a dynamic super-circuit, but it is not a generated monolith. It is a retained composition of independent providers whose contracts and tests remain intact.</p>
<p>The scheduler orders circuit stages by declared reads and writes and runs them deterministically. Concrete execution produces the actual task result. Abstract preflight and symbolic decision coverage may analyze the same circuit model for convergence, possible statuses, decision-row reachability, and bounded path coverage, but they do not replace the concrete verdict. A successful symbolic projection is evidence about the circuit; it is not the answer to the operator.</p>

<h2>The system preserves distinctions that ordinary text often blurs</h2>
<p>A sentence in a source is normally an attributed claim, not automatically a world fact. Failing to find a claim is not the same as finding its negation; a circuit may conclude from absence only when compatible coverage establishes that the relevant source scope was complete. Two possible references or scopes remain alternative interpretations when choosing one would change the result. Predicate logic retains true, false, unknown, and conflict, while rule findings add states such as not applicable, accepted exception, blocked ontology, or blocked coverage.</p>
<p>These distinctions affect the public answer. In the cold-chain task, the expired calibration and denied receiving-party acknowledgement can support a violation. The excursion path remains unresolved when the source does not establish the condition needed to decide it. The renderer must say those things in domain sentences; it may not expose an implementation constant such as <code>EXCURSION_QUARANTINE_PATH</code> as though that were an explanation.</p>

<h2>The public answer is controlled prose, not a debug dump</h2>
<p>Semantic circuits emit findings and typed frames. Response circuits receive those immutable values and apply IntentJS presentation policy. They suppress non-applicable and internal checks, retain material results, group compatible findings, count them, select evidence already attached by the semantic circuit, and render <code>results/response.md</code>. They cannot change a status, invent a source span, or repair missing semantics in the presentation layer.</p>
<p>The answer is Markdown CNL: readable sentences with stable markers that a later tool or LLM can filter without parsing arbitrary prose. Requirement codes remain in technical artifacts or explicit machine tags; public requirement lists contain complete domain statements. Generated explanation and copied source text are visibly separated. Every evidence block announces that it is copied exactly, names its source, and reproduces the verified substring rather than asking the reader to resolve character coordinates.</p>

<h2>Acceptance depends on executable evidence</h2>
<p>Codex completing a process is not proof that the authored semantics are correct. Ontology checks validate identities and frames. LongText checks validate exact anchors, polarity, modality, alternatives, and coverage. Circuit microcases exercise satisfied, violated, unknown, conflict, and not-applicable behavior. Planner tests establish selection and dependency closure. Response tests establish that the public answer contains the selected findings and exact evidence without leaking raw assurance structures.</p>
<p>An evaluation that claims natural-language authoring must also retain the exact brief, instruction, source, installed skills, generated context, Codex logs, created modules, focused tests, execution artifacts, and replay result. A second ordinary run must reproduce the semantic result and public response without Codex. This is why the tutorial pages show Input, Intermediate semantic programs, and Output together: the useful claim is not merely that a model once answered correctly, but that the answer now follows from inspectable code and verified evidence.</p>`
);

const architecture = page(
  "architecture.html",
  "Architecture and Execution Model",
  "How authored meaning becomes a deterministic composed runtime",
  `<p class="lead">The architecture is organized around a change of authority. Natural language begins as material that can support several interpretations. A coding agent may choose and encode an interpretation, but once the semantic modules are committed, the runtime—not the model conversation—owns execution. Every layer exists to make that transfer explicit without reducing the DSLs to inert configuration.</p>

<h2>Authoring crosses the ambiguous boundary once</h2>
<p>Before authoring, nllAgent has source bytes, a task instruction, reusable knowledge, and a catalog of constructors and circuits. It does not yet have an executable account of what each relevant sentence means for this task. A generic parser could manufacture records from words, but such records would hide decisions about identity, scope, modality, negation, completeness, and domain vocabulary. Those decisions are precisely where natural-language understanding is difficult.</p>
<p>The coding agent is therefore permitted to use full JavaScript while it creates OntologyJS, IntentJS, LongTextJS, CircuitJS, response policy, and tests. Full JavaScript is not a loophole around the semantic model. The SDK constructors are the boundary through which terms, claims, circuit stages, and results become semantic values. Procedural helpers may calculate, loop, recurse, or call local tools, but semantic effects must still pass through sealed builders, transactions, declared circuit outputs, and validation.</p>
<p>After authoring, ordinary execution imports those modules in a fresh process. The runtime is not allowed to revisit the source prose and make a new undocumented interpretation. If a required concept or behavior is absent, planning produces a typed gap. An explicit coding phase may fill it; deterministic execution may not improvise around it.</p>

<h2>The SDK gives every DSL a common semantic algebra</h2>
<p>The four main languages share a many-sorted algebra. Broad sorts such as entity, event, state, proposition, claim, quantity, time, evidence, scope, finding, and intent define the kinds of values that can participate in the system. An ontology extends that signature with domain concepts and roles. Calling a constructor such as <code>CustodyTransfer(from(...), to(...))</code> creates an opaque semantic handle whose sort, ontology identity, roles, and provenance are validated. It does not return a mutable object whose keys happen to look meaningful.</p>
<p>This common signature is what lets the layers connect without sharing storage assumptions. OntologyJS defines a constructor. LongTextJS calls it with ground values from a source. CircuitJS calls the same semantic family with variables or patterns. IntentJS asks for a capability that consumes or produces those meanings. Response code receives a typed finding whose evidence refers back to values created under the same identities.</p>
<p>Identity is semantic rather than accidental object identity. Two mentions with identical text at different source spans remain distinct. Two quantities can canonicalize to the same magnitude and dimension. Claims remain distinct when voice, polarity, scope, grounding, or interpretation differs. Circuit instances include their template, bindings, ontology stack, source snapshot, and interpretation context. These choices allow caching and replay without erasing distinctions that can change a verdict.</p>

<h2>A transaction turns authored source meaning into store truth</h2>
<p>LongTextJS may construct values procedurally, so the runtime obtains integrity at commit time. A semantic transaction binds itself to the source snapshot, ontology stack, and interpretation context. It collects provisional terms and claims, resolves references, checks sorts and role cardinalities, verifies grounding and coverage, canonicalizes identities, and commits atomically. If the source says a thermometer certificate expired, the resulting claim is not accepted merely because a JavaScript object was created; it must use the correct domain roles, polarity, source span, and ontology identities.</p>
<p>The committed snapshot becomes one logical <code>SemanticStore</code>. The store is a typed attributed term graph with claims, voices, scopes, alternatives, time, quantities, coverage, derived values, findings, and provenance. Its physical indexes are chosen for execution: type and subtype lookup, outgoing and reverse roles, claims by content, source intervals, temporal constraints, quantities, aliases, dependencies, and capabilities. These indexes can be rebuilt from the source modules and disposable caches. They never become a second semantic database that a circuit must understand.</p>
<p>Queries return rows that carry bindings, scope, interpretation conditions, evidence, and completeness metadata. This matters because a join between values from incompatible alternatives must not pretend that both are simultaneously true. It also matters for negative operations: <code>notExists</code>, <code>none</code>, and <code>all</code> require a coverage witness for the same concept, scope, source set, and assumptions. Without it, the result remains unknown or blocked by coverage.</p>

<h2>Intent converts purpose into a demand for capabilities</h2>
<p>The source interpretation does not determine what the operator wants. The same semantic store can support chronology review, evidence review, policy validation, explanation, repair, or controlled generation. IntentJS records mode, target, concerns, scope, evidence policy, allowed guarantees, output kinds, resource policy, and fallback. It retains the original instruction as provenance while replacing the unstructured prompt with an immutable executable model.</p>
<p>Intent fragments may come from explicit CLI requirements, the task instruction, task-local declarations, agent defaults, source signals, and earlier diagnostics. Their precedence is deterministic. Lexical or structural signals can suggest that a document is normative, scientific, or narrative, but signals do not themselves produce findings. If several domains are plausible, compatible packs can coexist. If no narrowing is justified, the broad fallback executes every compatible circuit in the resolved registry rather than silently doing almost nothing.</p>
<p>The planner begins at the requested outputs and concerns. A finding capability can have several provider circuits; a chosen provider can require several other capabilities. This creates an AND/OR closure problem. Hard incompatibilities, missing ontology, forbidden approximation, and unmet coverage are resolved before cost ranking. Selected providers are ordered by guarantees, completeness, robustness, determinism, reuse, cost, and finally stable identity. The execution plan records not just what will run, but why alternatives were rejected or blocked.</p>

<h2>The effective super-circuit remains a composition of small programs</h2>
<p>A selected circuit is a contract with one semantic responsibility. It declares requirements, provisions, stages, possible outputs, and optional assurance adapters. One circuit may include another directly, depend on a capability supplied elsewhere, instantiate once per semantic match, request an assurance circuit, or emit a refinement demand. The planner combines those relationships into an effective runtime without copying them into one opaque generated file.</p>
<p>Inside the composition, each declarative stage has typed inputs and outputs. The scheduler builds a dependency graph from those reads and writes. A stable ready queue executes nodes whose inputs are available, validates their outputs, records trace events, and activates newly ready dependents. Procedural stages are macro-nodes: they may contain ordinary algorithms internally, but their external semantic reads, writes, diagnostics, and optional abstract or symbolic summaries remain declared. This is the point at which SOLID ownership becomes operational rather than stylistic: a measurement normalizer, acknowledgement assessor, release-support decision, and response selector can evolve independently while composing through explicit values.</p>
<p>New derived values or per-match circuit instances can extend the graph during an epoch. The instance key prevents duplicate work. When a source unit changes, reverse dependencies identify affected materialization, queries, and descendants; unchanged values may be reused through content identities. A new source snapshot or non-monotone change creates a new epoch rather than mutating the meaning of an old replay.</p>

<h2>Concrete execution answers the task; auxiliary interpreters inspect the program</h2>
<p>Concrete execution evaluates ground terms from the current store and produces the actual findings or frames. It is the truth-bearing task interpretation. Abstract preflight propagates conservative domains such as possible statuses, evidence states, identity relations, intervals, scopes, and coverage. It can expose that a circuit may reach conflict or that an opaque stage loses precision without enumerating all concrete inputs.</p>
<p>Symbolic execution explores registered branch conditions and decision rows over symbolic values. It is deliberately aimed at semantic circuit constructs rather than arbitrary JavaScript instructions. The constraint kernel can reject infeasible paths and generate bounded microcases around decisions. The resulting path set establishes coverage of the circuit logic; it does not say which symbolic path the current document took. That distinction is why assurance lives in technical artifacts while the public answer is built from concrete findings.</p>
<p>Other compact methods—temporal networks, relation fixed points, finite-domain constraints, monitor-style transition checks, rewriting, decision DAGs, slicing, or bounded repair—are selected by problem shape. They are cooperating kernels over the same handles, not competing stores or a claim of universal theorem proving. Every method advertises applicability and guarantee; approximate output remains separate from four-valued semantic truth.</p>

<h2>Response composition is a second program boundary</h2>
<p>Semantic execution can legitimately produce more information than the operator should read: internal grounding confirmations, non-applicable branches, secondary satisfied checks, raw paths, and generated frames unrelated to the requested mode. Response circuits operate on immutable findings and frames after semantic execution. Their stages select intent-visible entries, group them, count them, rank attached evidence, and choose a presentation style. The composer validates after every stage that no finding or frame was invented.</p>
<p>The semantic circuit owns the meaning of a requirement and must attach a human-readable statement when it also uses an internal code. The renderer owns fixed grammar such as “the following required conditions are not satisfied.” It cannot discover that <code>THERMOMETER_CALIBRATION_VALID</code> means validity at the transfer time by splitting underscores. The evidence section likewise consumes verified spans selected by the semantic result. It may quote them and mark them as copied source text; it may not search the raw source to repair missing evidence.</p>

<h2>Failures remain in the layer that can explain them</h2>
<p>A process failure means a command did not complete. A semantic violation means the command completed and found a problem. Unknown means the evidence cannot decide a proposition under the declared scope. Conflict means compatible support and refutation coexist. Blocked ontology, coverage, resource, or method states name the dependency that prevented a requested result. These statuses are kept separate so a tool exit code cannot masquerade as semantic truth.</p>
<p>Every stage records its inputs, outputs, source and ontology dependencies, circuit or method identity, interpretation context, decision reason, cache use, diagnostics, and cost metadata. A backward slice can then show exactly which source claims influenced one finding. Replaying the same modules in a fresh process checks that the result is a consequence of committed code and source snapshots, not of scheduler accident or stale model state. The <a href="runtime.html">runtime reference</a> enumerates the implemented constructors and commands; this chapter supplies the causal model that makes those details intelligible.</p>`
);

const agenticAuthoring = page(
  "agentic-authoring.html",
  "Agentic Natural-Language Authoring",
  "How briefs and source texts become canonical semantic programs",
  `<p class="lead">Agentic authoring is the model-dependent half of nllAgent. It exists because an instruction and document do not arrive with their intended entities, scopes, rules, evidence relations, or completeness assumptions already marked. Codex resolves those choices by writing inspectable programs under explicit ownership. The runtime then validates and executes the programs independently.</p>

<h2>Authoring begins by deciding what should survive this task</h2>
<p>The first architectural decision is ownership. Suppose a brief asks for an operational-policy reviewer that can detect contradictory rules, missing exception justifications, unsupported conclusions, and requests for generated procedures. Those concepts and behaviors are useful across many documents. They belong to the agent: the ontology can define operational rules, invocations, justifications, support relations, and procedure requests; reusable circuits can analyze those meanings without knowing the text of any future case.</p>
<p>A concrete task then says “check this exception” and supplies a policy plus an invocation record. The exact invocation, its quoted policy, its missing justification, and the task's desired evidence-led answer belong only to that task. IntentJS records the requested review. LongTextJS creates the source-grounded rule, invocation, and denied or absent justification. If these facts were written into the reusable agent ontology, a later document could inherit them as default knowledge. If the reusable exception algorithm were copied into every task, review and calibration would fragment. Agentic authoring separates those two mistakes before code is written.</p>

<h2>An agent brief teaches vocabulary and behavior, not source facts</h2>
<p>Agent-level phases start from a retained natural-language brief. The architecture phase decides which meanings, capabilities, profiles, response policies, and tests form one coherent agent. The ontology phase gives stable identities and typed constructors to the reusable meanings. The circuit phase writes checks and generation procedures against patterns over those meanings. A response phase may add audience-specific grouping or controlled vocabulary when framework defaults are insufficient. Tests define microcases that must continue to hold across tasks.</p>
<p>The resulting agent folder is a persistent semantic package. Its code is expected to survive sources that use different names and sentence structures because circuits operate over semantic handles rather than strings. A contradiction circuit compares grounded action, condition, scope, and effect. An exception circuit asks whether an invocation is governed by a policy and connected to a grounded justification record. Their lexical manifestations are handled during LongText authoring; their decision semantics remain reusable.</p>

<h2>A task instruction chooses an operation; the source supplies its evidence</h2>
<p>Task-level authoring keeps the instruction separate from the document. Codex reads the instruction to create IntentJS: analysis or generation mode, concerns, target scope, evidence discipline, requested outputs, assurance, and presentation directives. It reads decoded source text to create LongTextJS: mentions, entities, events, claims, voices, modalities, polarities, contexts, alternatives, quantities, times, identities, and coverage. The original instruction and source remain retained alongside those programs, so a reviewer can compare the interpretation with what was actually asked and written.</p>
<p>LongText authoring is not a transcription exercise. “The operator may bypass inspection during an emergency, provided the invocation records a justification” contains a permission, an exception condition, and a required justification relation. “No justification was recorded” is an explicit denial, not merely failure to see a positive sentence. A later circuit can reach a violation for the right reason only if those distinctions were materialized and grounded at exact spans.</p>
<p>Task-local ontology or circuits are exceptional but legitimate. A cold-chain source can introduce calibration-at-transfer, excursion quarantine, and stability-study support that a core-only agent cannot express. Adaptive authoring adds those meanings and the release-support circuit under the task. The code is not automatically promoted after one success; promotion requires a separate reusable contract and calibration across independent cases.</p>

<h2>nllAgent prepares an evidence-rich coding workspace</h2>
<p>The framework does not paste the repository into one enormous prompt. It resolves the selected phase's skill dependency graph and materializes exactly the context artifacts declared by that closure. An ontology phase needs the SDK and resolved ontology catalogs. A circuit phase also needs circuit and response catalogs, diagnostics, and runtime contracts. A task grounding phase needs the source outline, exact decoded source, active ontology, profile resolution, and intent. Every catalog is generated from live project objects and points back to canonical modules; none replaces the code it describes.</p>
<p>The run directory receives dependency-ordered skills, exact DS contracts, context artifacts, <code>INSTRUCTIONS.md</code>, and an executable run manifest. The instructions name the canonical working directory and edit owner. Codex reads the contracts and catalogs, opens deeper source as needed, and edits the agent or task directly. The run directory retains context and logs; it is not a staging copy from which nllAgent later reconstructs semantic files.</p>

<h2>Skills divide one coding lifecycle into bounded responsibilities</h2>
<p>Each project skill pairs human operational instructions in <code>SKILL.md</code> with an executable <code>workflow.mjs</code>. The workflow declares specifications, context artifacts, CLI tools, dependencies, edit roots, and applicable phases. Dependencies establish the reading and installation order: a circuit author receives runtime and ontology contracts before circuit-specific instructions, while a LongText author receives intent and ontology context before grounding the source.</p>
<p>The separation is about responsibility, not artificial isolation. The architect may identify that an ontology and four circuits are needed. The ontology author may expose constructors used by the circuit author. The task intent author may select a capability supplied by those circuits. The LongText author may discover a missing role and route a typed refinement back to ontology work. Each phase sees enough shared semantic context to make those handoffs explicit, while edit roots prevent a task grounding phase from silently redesigning framework primitives.</p>
<p><a href="coding-agent.html">The coding-agent chapter</a> examines what Codex sees and does across these phases. <a href="skills-workflow.html">How coding skills work</a> then exposes the live manifest values and verification workflow of every individual skill.</p>

<h2>Completion of a Codex process is only the start of acceptance</h2>
<p>The adapter records that Codex started, which directory it used, which instructions it received, what it printed, which files changed, when it finished, and its exit status. That proves an authoring process occurred. It does not prove that a role range is correct, a source anchor still matches, a decision table covers unknown and conflict, or the public response quotes decisive evidence.</p>
<p>Phase-specific checks therefore inspect the authored artifact. Ontology modules must import, seal, and preserve identity closure. Intent must retain the instruction and request actual capabilities and output policy. LongText must commit and verify every source anchor. Circuits must declare dependencies, emit evidence-bearing results, and pass focused microcases. Response behavior must preserve selected findings while excluding internal projections. Tests execute without Codex, so a second process judges artifacts rather than trusting the author's report.</p>

<h2>Real evaluation proves authoring rather than fixture replay</h2>
<p>A committed example can prove runtime behavior, but it cannot prove that a coding agent created the semantics from natural language. The agentic evaluation begins with an isolated folder that lacks the target reusable programs. It retains the exact brief, invokes Codex for the agent phases, creates random-ID tasks, invokes Codex for task intent and grounding, and records the created code and every phase run. Each case must then pass semantic expectations and reproduce its result under ordinary model-free replay.</p>
<p>The four rule-review cases deliberately demand different behaviors: a real contradiction, a missing justification, an unsupported conclusion, and controlled procedure generation. Strict oracles reject unexpected material findings as well as missing expected ones. Exact decisive quotations are part of acceptance, so a correct label with weak evidence is not enough. Failed attempts remain retained; a later repair is another explicit run, not a rewritten history.</p>

<h2>Adaptive authoring extends the same contract inside one difficult task</h2>
<p>Ordinary tasks should reuse a reviewed agent. When inherited ontology or circuits cannot express the requested operation, <code>analyze --author-adaptive</code> makes the exception explicit. Codex audits intent, ontology, grounding, circuit capability, response policy, and tests under task ownership. The planner composes inherited and local providers. Deterministic acceptance requires concrete material output, valid anchors, focused tests, qualitative CNL, requested abstract and symbolic evidence, and equivalent replay. A mandatory review examines even an initially successful run for overfitting, missing unknown behavior, weak provenance, or empty assurance.</p>
<p>Once accepted, the task can run again without Codex. This is the architectural endpoint of authoring: the flexible model has left behind code precise enough that the system can explain, test, and repeat what it now means by the original request.</p>`
);

const codingAgent = page(
  "coding-agent.html",
  "The Coding Agent Inside nllAgent",
  "Adapter, skills, context, direct editing, and independent acceptance",
  `<p class="lead">The coding agent is the component that converts an underspecified natural-language authoring request into canonical semantic code. It is not the semantic agent stored in <code>agents/&lt;name&gt;/</code>, and it is not the deterministic runtime. Codex is the first implementation of this role; <code>CodingAgentAdapter</code> keeps the process boundary replaceable.</p>

<h2>Three actors with similar names do different work</h2>
<p>A <em>semantic agent</em> is a persistent folder of reusable ontology, circuits, profiles, response behavior, and tests. A <em>coding agent</em> is a process that reads natural language and edits those folders or one task folder. nllAgent itself is the orchestrator and runtime: it resolves ownership, prepares context, starts the coding process when explicitly requested, then validates and executes the resulting modules. Confusing these actors makes it appear either that an agent folder learned by itself or that ordinary execution secretly calls a model.</p>
<p>Codex may run during <code>code ...</code>, real authoring evaluation, or adaptive authoring. It never runs merely because the user requested <code>run</code>, <code>plan</code>, <code>query</code>, source inspection, or unit tests. This makes model use observable and gives ordinary replay a clear meaning.</p>

<h2>The adapter creates a process, not a semantic oracle</h2>
<p><code>CodexAdapter</code> invokes the locally installed <code>codex exec</code> command in direct-editing mode. It sets the canonical agent or task directory as the working directory and points Codex to file-based instructions. An optional model identifier is passed through when the operator supplies one; otherwise the local Codex configuration chooses its default. Repository code does not hard-code a time-sensitive model name.</p>
<p>The adapter captures standard output and error, final response, start and finish times, exit status, and changed paths. A zero exit status means the process completed. The adapter does not parse the final prose as a finding, convert it into patches, or decide that tests are semantically sufficient. That deliberate lack of authority is what allows a future adapter to replace Codex without changing the meaning of accepted nllAgent artifacts.</p>

<h2>Before Codex starts, nllAgent constructs the world it may rely on</h2>
<p>The orchestrator first resolves the project root, target agent, optional task, authoring phase, canonical edit owner, and write lock. It decodes task sources and resolves the active profile plus framework, agent, and task semantic modules. It then loads the selected skill's executable workflow and closes its dependencies. The dependency closure decides both which instructions are installed and which context projections exist.</p>
<p>A run does not receive generic copied theory. <code>SDK_CATALOG.md</code> is derived from live SDK descriptors and shows current imports, signatures, semantics, examples, and diagnostics. <code>ONTOLOGY_CATALOG.md</code> reports the resolved concepts, roles, relations, constructors, and pack ownership. <code>CIRCUIT_CATALOG.md</code> and <code>RESPONSE_CIRCUIT_CATALOG.md</code> report providers, requirements, provisions, stages, statuses, assurance, and local override identities. <code>PROFILE_RESOLUTION.md</code> explains loaded packs and precedence. <code>SOURCE_OUTLINE.md</code> and source tools expose decoded units and exact text. <code>DIAGNOSTICS.md</code> gives the current failures that a review must repair. <code>PROJECT_MAP.md</code> locates canonical owners without assuming a fixed workspace URL.</p>
<p>Only artifacts named by the dependency-closed workflows are generated. This is important in both directions. Missing a declared catalog is a configuration error, because Codex would be asked to code without a promised dependency. Generating every catalog for every phase would create a large, misleading context and make workflow manifests descriptive rather than executable.</p>

<h2>A skill is both a reading contract and a machine-resolved manifest</h2>
<p>Each <code>nll-*</code> skill has two synchronized parts. <code>SKILL.md</code> tells Codex when the skill applies, which design boundaries matter, how to inspect the current state, which artifacts it may create, what tests it must write, and what evidence completes the phase. <code>workflow.mjs</code> declares the same responsibility in a form nllAgent can resolve: design specifications, context artifacts, CLI tools, dependencies, edit roots, and lifecycle phases.</p>
<p>Dependencies create a meaningful order rather than a flat pile of prompts. The ontology skill depends on SDK knowledge because it must use real constructors. LongText work depends on intent and ontology because grounding must reflect both the operation and the available semantic signature. Circuit work depends on runtime and ontology because it must choose query, decision, procedural, or assurance primitives that the local runtime actually implements. Testing and evaluation close the chain without giving test execution permission to start Codex implicitly.</p>
<p>The generated <code>INSTRUCTIONS.md</code> makes that order explicit: installed dependency skill contracts first, then the selected DS files, then the context artifacts that were actually generated, followed by the objective, edit root, and checks. The adjacent <code>run.mjs</code> records the adapter, working directory, skill IDs, owner, and deterministic owner-level command. A reviewer can therefore reconstruct what Codex was told without relying on ephemeral API messages.</p>

<h2>Codex edits the source of truth directly</h2>
<p>The canonical working directory is the agent when the phase creates reusable knowledge and the task when it interprets one source. Codex may inspect any relevant local code, but the workflow's edit roots communicate which ownership boundary it must preserve. It writes ordinary <code>.mjs</code> modules using SDK builders, imports semantic identities from resolved ontology facades, and writes <code>node:test</code> checks beside the owner. nllAgent does not ask for JSON and later translate it into DSL code; such translation would erase the design choices and make the retained model output the hidden authority.</p>
<p>During architecture work, Codex decides which modules and capabilities are needed and where they belong. During ontology work, it designs stable concepts, roles, cardinalities, lexicalizations, and constructor facades. During circuit work, it implements source-independent behavior and its satisfied, violated, unknown, conflict, and not-applicable microcases. During task intent work, it preserves the original instruction and declares presentation policy. During LongText work, it uses source inspection tools to choose exact spans and constructs claims, contexts, alternatives, and coverage. Review work receives failures and trace slices and may repair the canonical modules; it cannot weaken the acceptance gates that requested the review.</p>

<h2>Tools give the coding agent observations, not answers to copy</h2>
<p>CLI tools reduce uncertainty about the local system. Catalog commands reveal which constructors and providers exist. Source commands expose exact decoded evidence. Intent and plan commands explain selection. Ontology, LongText, and circuit checks expose typed contract violations. Trace commands isolate the dependencies of a bad finding. These tools do not author semantic meaning on Codex's behalf; they make the state that constrains authoring observable.</p>
<pre><code># Build the dependency-closed skills and live context for an intent-authoring phase without starting Codex.
node nllAgent.mjs context build --agent-dir path/to/agent --task-dir path/to/task --phase intent

# Inspect the exact skills, DS contracts, catalogs, owner, and checks placed in that prepared run.
node nllAgent.mjs context show --agent-dir path/to/agent --task-dir path/to/task --phase intent

# Start the explicit Codex intent-authoring phase against the canonical task directory.
node nllAgent.mjs code intent --agent-dir path/to/agent --task-dir path/to/task

# Execute the authored semantic programs afterward without invoking Codex.
node nllAgent.mjs run --agent-dir path/to/agent --task-dir path/to/task --assurance all</code></pre>

<h2>Verification is deliberately split across three viewpoints</h2>
<p>First, Codex runs the focused discovery and validation commands named by its skill while it works. This catches errors quickly and lets it iterate in context. Second, the generated run manifest names an owner-level fast test that nllAgent can execute after the process. Third, evaluation or adaptive authoring applies phase-specific acceptance: it compares before and after paths, imports the new code, verifies ontology closure and anchors, runs focused tests, executes real planning and circuits, checks public CNL and exact evidence, and requires replay.</p>
<p>These layers answer different questions. Codex's check asks whether the current edit appears coherent. The owner test asks whether the canonical folder still satisfies its local contract. Evaluation asks whether natural-language authoring actually created the requested behavior and whether that behavior survives model-free execution. A phase can pass the first and fail the third; the retained failure is useful evidence about the coding workflow rather than something to hide.</p>

<h2>Retained runs make the coding process auditable</h2>
<p>Every run directory keeps installed skills, generated context, instructions, process logs, final response, timings, exit status, before/after artifact inventory, and check results. In a real authoring evaluation, the agent brief and task sources are retained outside those run directories as input evidence. The canonical modules live under the agent or task. Results and replay live under the task. These separate locations let a reviewer answer four different questions: what Codex was asked, what it knew, what it changed, and what the independent runtime subsequently proved.</p>
<p>The <a href="skills-workflow.html">skill workflow chapter</a> exposes every live manifest and its exact tool set. The <a href="testing-evaluation.html">testing and evaluation chapter</a> explains the independent gates. The <a href="tutorials.html">tutorials</a> show the application-facing result after this process has produced accepted code.</p>`
);

const projectStructure = page(
  "project-structure.html",
  "Project Folders and Ownership",
  "How repository placement preserves authority, reuse, and evidence",
  `<p class="lead">The repository layout is not housekeeping around the semantic system; it is part of the semantic contract. A module's directory says whether it is framework machinery, default reusable knowledge, a specialized agent capability, an interpretation of one source, coding-process evidence, or a public result. The runtime and authoring tools use those ownership boundaries when they resolve dependencies and decide what may be reused.</p>

<h2>The root separates original contracts from executable implementation</h2>
<p><code>design-specifications/</code> contains the original DS-000 through DS-019 source contracts. Their content remains preserved rather than rewritten into shorter summaries. <code>docs/specs/</code> contains the contiguous official set: wrappers around the preserved originals plus additive implementation contracts discovered while building the system. <code>AGENTS.md</code> gives repository-wide coding and ownership rules. <code>observations.md</code> is the review index for decisions that still deserve discussion; <code>insights.md</code> records recurring problem classes observed in real coding-agent work and the regression controls added after them.</p>
<p><code>tools/</code> owns deterministic project generators and verifiers: official specifications, HTML, source maps, context catalogs, retained tutorial projections, and contract checks. Generated documentation lives under <code>docs/</code> with document-relative navigation and project-owned assets. Environment-managed <code>.agents/</code> may contain maintenance skills available to the current Codex session, but it is not nllAgent product source, is not read by documentation generation, and is not published as a runtime dependency.</p>

<h2>The framework defines the languages and their execution, not a particular agent</h2>
<p><code>framework/sdk/</code> contains the public language surfaces. Core modules define handles, identity, values, collections, logic, provenance, and diagnostics. Ontology, LongText, Intent, Circuit, CNL, agent, and evaluation modules provide fluent builders for their respective contracts. Code here must remain domain-independent enough that a cold-chain task and a narrative-continuity task can use the same transaction, claim, query, decision, evidence, and response primitives.</p>
<p><code>framework/runtime/</code> consumes those values. Its store owns transactions, canonicalization, immutable snapshots, and indexes. Query code compiles semantic patterns without exposing physical storage. The planner closes capabilities and explains selection. The scheduler orders declared dataflow. Method kernels implement concrete and auxiliary algorithms. Response composition validates presentation stages. Trace and cache code retain dependencies and replay metadata. This layer may know what a <code>Finding</code> or <code>SourceSpan</code> is; it must not know that one task's thermometer is TH-9.</p>
<p><code>framework/tools/</code> connects semantic libraries to the filesystem and coding processes. It resolves agent and task folders, decodes source files, builds run context, invokes adapters, imports semantic programs, writes results, and runs evaluation. <code>framework/cli/</code> routes user commands to those tools. Keeping the CLI thin matters because evaluation and tests must be able to call the same reusable implementation without scraping terminal text.</p>

<h2>Packs and profiles provide reusable defaults before an agent adds a specialty</h2>
<p><code>framework/packs/</code> contains versioned ontology and circuit modules for common language, logic, elementary domains, reasoning errors, normative structure, and social interaction. A pack can declare concepts, roles, relations, facts, laws, recognition signals, capabilities, requirements, possible statuses, and assurance support. Pack facts are explicitly pack-owned; they are never presented as claims extracted from the current task source.</p>
<p><code>profiles/</code> choose coherent pack sets and fallback policies. A minimal profile makes debugging and isolation possible. A broad school profile loads the intended baseline knowledge. A legal or literary profile gives domain-specific emphasis. The profile does not hard-code the final circuit list: IntentJS and capability planning still filter by target, concern, ontology, evidence, output, and guarantee. An agent may provide a local profile when its specialty needs a composition that should travel with that agent.</p>

<h2>Project skills teach coding phases how to use the live system</h2>
<p><code>nll-skills/</code> contains the project-owned authoring workflows. They are not semantic knowledge packs and they are not semantic agents. They teach a coding agent how to create or review architecture, orchestration, SDK primitives, runtime behavior, intent, ontology, LongText grounding, circuits, tests, and evaluations. Each folder owns <code>SKILL.md</code> and <code>workflow.mjs</code>; the latter lets nllAgent resolve dependencies, context, tools, edit roots, and phases programmatically.</p>
<p>The theory these skills need remains authoritative in the DS files and SDK or pack code. A run-local catalog projects the live parts needed for one phase. This prevents ten copied descriptions of the same constructor from drifting while still giving Codex enough context to choose real imports and reuse existing identities.</p>

<h2>An agent folder is a reusable semantic product</h2>
<p>An agent can live under <code>agents/&lt;name&gt;/</code>, <code>examples/</code>, <code>evaluations/</code>, or an explicit directory supplied to the CLI. Its location may differ, but its ownership model is the same. <code>agent.mjs</code> composes framework packs, a default profile, local modules, and coding policy. <code>ontologies/</code> defines reusable vocabulary. <code>circuits/</code> and <code>methods/</code> define reusable behavior. <code>cnl/</code> can define agent-specific response composition. <code>profiles/</code> and <code>lexicons/</code> refine selection and language. <code>tests/</code> calibrates these capabilities independently of any one source.</p>
<p>An agent also owns its <code>tasks/</code> collection and may retain agent-level authoring runs. Code placed at the agent level is available to every task resolved through that agent. That is why a coding phase must not promote a document's named entities or one-off conclusions into the agent merely because doing so makes the current test pass.</p>

<h2>A task folder is one request, one source snapshot, and its complete audit trail</h2>
<p><code>task.mjs</code> identifies the task sources, persistent instruction, expected language, and optional profile overlays. <code>source/</code> retains original files, deterministic decoded source maps, and any task-owned extractor. <code>intent/</code> owns the executable interpretation of the request. <code>longtext/</code> owns the executable interpretation of the source, often split into stable units and a deterministic root commit.</p>
<p><code>ontologies/</code> and <code>circuits/</code> appear under a task only when the inherited system cannot represent a needed distinction or operation. <code>cnl/</code> can add task-local response behavior without changing semantic truth. <code>sdk/</code> may contain generated facades bound to the resolved ontology identities. <code>tests/</code> covers this interpretation, including source anchors and local decision behavior. These files are Intermediate artifacts in the tutorials because they are the inspectable bridge from natural-language input to output.</p>
<p><code>runs/</code> retains how coding work occurred. Each run has installed skills, generated context, instructions, adapter logs, final response, and checks. <code>results/response.md</code> is the public Markdown CNL answer. Its siblings are technical evidence: execution plan, canonical findings and frames, executable result modules, response-circuit trace, diagnostics, coverage, assurance, binary trace, and artifact manifest. Keeping public and technical results together under the task preserves discoverability without mixing them into one unreadable answer.</p>

<h2>The anatomy of a task mirrors the transformation it records</h2>
<pre><code>tasks/task-ID/
  task.mjs                 instruction and source ownership
  source/                  original bytes, decoded map, optional extractors
  intent/intent.mjs        requested operation and presentation policy
  longtext/                grounded claims, contexts, alternatives, coverage
  ontologies/              only meanings missing from inherited knowledge
  circuits/                only behavior missing from inherited providers
  cnl/                     optional task response composition
  sdk/                     generated constructor facade for resolved identities
  tests/                   anchors, semantics, decisions, response checks
  runs/run-ID/             skills, live context, Codex process, checks
  results/response.md      primary human-facing Markdown CNL
  results/artifacts.md     index of semantic programs and technical evidence
  results/                 plan, findings, frames, diagnostics, trace, assurance</code></pre>
<p>The order in this tree is causal rather than merely conventional. The source map must exist before exact LongText spans can verify. Ontology identities must resolve before LongText and Circuit modules can import their constructors. Intent must request capabilities before the planner can select providers. Semantic findings must exist before response circuits can render the public answer. Runs and results point back to the canonical modules instead of becoming alternate sources of truth.</p>

<h2>Resolution builds one stack from several owners</h2>
<p>Every semantic command names an agent through <code>--agent</code> or <code>--agent-dir</code>; task commands additionally use <code>--task</code> or <code>--task-dir</code>. Names resolve through conventional folders, while explicit directories let a caller keep agents elsewhere. Generated import specifiers are calculated relative to each target file and the selected project root, so documentation and semantic code do not need machine-specific absolute URLs.</p>
<p>The runtime begins with mandatory framework language, adds the selected profile's packs, then agent-local ontology and circuits, and finally task-local intent, grounding, and extensions. A later same-identity module may replace an earlier module only where an explicit override contract permits it. Otherwise providers coexist and the planner chooses by capability. Run-local context is informative and never outranks canonical code.</p>

<h2>Examples and evaluations preserve different kinds of evidence</h2>
<p><code>examples/</code> contains committed executable agents and tasks used for explanation and regression. They prove that existing semantic programs run. <code>evaluations/</code> creates isolated agents and random-ID tasks to measure authoring, semantic accuracy, evidence fidelity, assurance, and replay. A real authoring evaluation proves that target modules were absent, retains the Codex phases that created them, and links metrics to the exact task folder. It therefore carries more process evidence than a stable example.</p>
<p><code>docs/</code> projects accepted artifacts into portable pages; it does not become their authority. Tutorial file viewers embed escaped retained content so a static documentation server never fetches arbitrary paths outside its tree. All navigation remains relative. The result is a repository in which a programmer can move from contract, to canonical source, to coding history, to execution evidence without confusing one layer for another.</p>`
);

const sourceIngestion = page(
  "source-ingestion.html",
  "Source Ingestion and Provenance",
  "How retained bytes become exact evidence without hidden interpretation",
  `<p class="lead">Source ingestion creates the stable textual substrate on which semantic authoring depends. It answers “what exact text was decoded, where is this passage, and has it changed?” It deliberately does not answer “what does this sentence mean for the task?” That second question belongs to LongTextJS authoring under an explicit coding-agent phase.</p>

<h2>Why provenance begins before semantic code</h2>
<p>A <code>SourceSpan</code> can support a finding only if the runtime can reproduce the text it names. Plain UTF-8 files make this look trivial, but even there a later edit can shift offsets or replace a selected phrase. PDFs complicate the problem further because visible text can be assembled from compressed streams, font mappings, and drawing operators rather than one contiguous byte range. Treating a byte offset or a string search as permanent evidence would make replay fragile.</p>
<p>nllAgent therefore defines decoded text as the coordinate space for semantic grounding. The source registry retains the original task-owned path, extractor identity and metadata, source digest, decoded-text digest, stable source units, and absolute decoded bounds. LongTextJS refers to that registry rather than opening arbitrary files. Anchor verification can then prove that a source ID, unit, interval, and selected-text hash still identify the passage the author intended.</p>

<h2>Ingestion chooses an explicit decoder and fails visibly</h2>
<p>The registry starts with the exact files declared by the task in stable lexical order. A task-owned module under <code>source/extractors/</code> has first opportunity to decode its known format. Otherwise built-in UTF-8 decoding handles text, Markdown, CNL, CSV, and HTML. The dependency-free PDF extractor validates the document, rejects encryption, decodes supported streams, interprets ordinary text operands, and retains page metadata.</p>
<p>A decoder must return reproducible text or a typed failure. Empty extraction, encrypted PDFs, unsupported filters, scans without OCR, custom fonts that cannot be decoded, invalid extractor modules, and unsupported formats do not become successful blank sources. A task may add an OCR capture or specialized font map through an explicit extractor, making that extra authority reviewable and replayable instead of pretending Node.js built-ins inferred the missing glyphs.</p>

<h2>Stable source units make large documents addressable</h2>
<p>Validated decoded text is segmented deterministically into source units such as paragraphs, sections, pages, or another stable task-defined structure. The executable <code>source/source-map.mjs</code> records source and unit identities, paths, digests, extractor metadata, and decoded bounds. It is executable project data rather than JSON, but it remains non-semantic: a heading boundary says where text is, not whether the heading defines a legal exception.</p>
<p>Source units help a coding agent navigate a long document without copying the entire file into every semantic module. A LongText unit can anchor several claims inside one source unit and export semantic handles. The root module commits units in deterministic order and reconciles cross-unit identity. If one unit changes, dependency tracking can invalidate the affected materialization while preserving unchanged evidence.</p>

<h2>The source tools expose evidence before Codex chooses its meaning</h2>
<pre><code># Decode the retained source files and rebuild the executable source map before semantic authoring.
node nllAgent.mjs source ingest \
  --agent-dir examples/validation-agent \
  --task task-symbolic-validation

# Inspect source units and decoded bounds so the author can navigate the exact retained text.
node nllAgent.mjs source outline \
  --agent-dir examples/validation-agent \
  --task task-symbolic-validation

# Locate every exact occurrence of “alarm” and obtain candidate decoded spans for grounding.
node nllAgent.mjs source search \
  --agent-dir examples/validation-agent \
  --task task-symbolic-validation \
  --text "alarm"

# Recheck all committed LongTextJS anchors after ingestion or any source revision.
node nllAgent.mjs source verify-anchors \
  --agent-dir examples/validation-agent \
  --task task-symbolic-validation</code></pre>
<p><code>source outline</code> and <code>source search</code> are authoring aids. They reveal exact retained text and coordinates; they do not decide that an alarm event precedes a gate-opening event. A coding agent uses those observations together with the task instruction, ontology constructors, and LongText skill to decide which semantic terms and claims the passages support.</p>

<h2>LongTextJS turns a passage into a grounded claim</h2>
<p>In the validation example, the source says that the alarm sounded in Building A at 08:57 and that Ana opened the north gate at 09:00. Ingestion produces decoded text and one stable source unit. LongTextJS then creates two typed facility events, their actors, locations, and times, wraps them as source claims, and attaches each claim to its exact sentence. The facility-order circuit later queries those values and compares time, but ingestion never asserts that the order is valid.</p>
<p>This separation becomes more important with negation and policy. In the cold-chain source, a search can locate “no acknowledgement by Vale Laboratory.” LongTextJS must decide that the passage grounds a denied transfer acknowledgement by the receiving party, not a generic negative sentence and not merely absence of a positive event. The ontology supplies the roles; the task instruction supplies the purpose; the source span supplies evidence. No one of those inputs is sufficient by itself.</p>

<h2>Anchor verification protects meaning from source drift</h2>
<p>A committed anchor contains the source identity, decoded digest, unit, start and end bounds, and selected-text hash. Verification checks all of them. If a revision inserts text earlier in the file, the old numeric interval cannot silently select different words. The coding agent may realign the claim explicitly and rerun its tests, producing a new source snapshot and semantic identity. If it does not, ordinary execution reports a stale or invalid span.</p>
<p>Coverage is a separate semantic assertion. Processing every source unit may justify a LongText declaration that a particular concept was covered for a particular scope, but the source map itself cannot claim that all exceptions or all acknowledgements were materialized. This distinction prevents a completed decoding pass from becoming a false closed-world guarantee.</p>

<h2>Exact input text remains distinct in the public answer</h2>
<p>Findings carry semantic evidence values. Provenance traversal reaches verified <code>SourceSpan</code> handles, and the response renderer uses their selected substrings. Machine offsets remain in technical artifacts for replay, while <code>response.md</code> reproduces the exact passage. The evidence section is marked as copied input, and each source quotation has its own source marker and relative link. Generated conclusions and requirement explanations remain outside the blockquote.</p>
<p>This visual and semantic boundary prevents two opposite confusions. A reader does not have to resolve “characters 42–77” to understand the evidence, and a later formatter does not mistake generated explanation for text written in the source. If decisive evidence is missing from the finding, the renderer is not allowed to rediscover it with a keyword search; the semantic circuit or LongText grounding must be repaired where the missing meaning belongs.</p>

<h2>A task-local extractor extends decoding without extending semantic authority</h2>
<p>A specialized binary format can add <code>source/extractors/bin.extractor.mjs</code>. Its function receives immutable path, extension, bytes, and task-root information and returns text plus reproducible metadata. The registry validates and hashes that text exactly like built-in output. The extractor may understand bytes and glyphs specific to that format; it still must not create ontology terms, IntentJS, findings, or circuit code.</p>
<pre><code>export function extractSource({ bytes }) {
  const text = decodeKnownCorpusFormat(bytes);
  return Object.freeze({
    text,
    metadata: Object.freeze({ format: "known-corpus-v1" })
  });
}</code></pre>
<p>The result is a clean chain of authority: the extractor establishes decoded evidence, the coding agent authors a semantic interpretation, the transaction validates that interpretation, circuits derive results, and response composition quotes only verified passages. Each step can be inspected and repaired without asking another layer to guess what the previous one omitted.</p>`
);

export function buildUnderstandDocumentationPages() {
  return Object.freeze([
    systemGuide,
    architecture,
    agenticAuthoring,
    codingAgent,
    projectStructure,
    sourceIngestion
  ]);
}
